- Department: Students from technical or academic departments (e.g., CSE, SWE, Pharmacy, EEE, Civil) are more likely to read academic books most of the time, whereas students from departments like BBA, English (if literature-focused), or JMC might read less academic books or more diverse genres.
- Amount of time spent reading books per day: More hours spent reading books per day likely indicates reading academic books if combined with other academic indicators.
- Reason for reading books: If the reason is "Academic Purpose," the student is likely reading academic books most of the time; other reasons suggest less academic reading.
- How to manage books for reading: Borrowing or buying books might indicate academic reading; e-books usage might be mixed.
- Number of e-books read last two years: Higher numbers suggest more reading activity, possibly academic if combined with other features.
- Number of printed books read last two years: Similarly, higher numbers suggest more reading activity.
- Favorite books for reading to spend leisure time: If leisure reading is mostly Fiction or Politics, the student might not be reading academic books most of the time; Non-Fiction or Biography might be closer to academic reading.
- What is the change felt after reading books: "Increase Academic Performance" strongly correlates with academic reading.
- Who influenced you to read books: Influence by Teachers or Self-Decision might correlate with academic reading; Friends or Parents less so.
- Which language books do you like to read: Preference for English might suggest academic reading, especially in technical departments.
- Where do you read printed books: Reading in Library or Home might suggest academic reading.
- Do you read Newspaper: Reading newspapers is not strongly related to academic book reading.
- The format used for reading books: Preference for Printed or Both formats might indicate academic reading.
- Frequency of reading books: Reading "On everyday basis" or "A few times a week" suggests more academic reading.

